# Setting Up a Video Project From Per-Act Audio

End-to-end workflow for standing up a new episode project from raw per-act
audio: transcribe → build subtitles → fix transcription errors → scaffold
per-act compositions (background + terrain + particles + subtitles) → register
→ verify. Distilled from the `7-education-ep3` (ep7), `11-education-ep4`
(ep4), and `13-wittgenstein-ai` (#13, prosody + dramatic beats) builds,
modeled on `6-time-money` (#6) and `5-education-ep2` (ep2).

> #13 (`13-wittgenstein-ai`) is the reference for **segment-level prosody**
> (per-`**bold**` pitch/rate), **`[停顿]`-driven baked silence**, the **dramatic
> setup→reveal beat**, and **display-only em-dash stripping** — all documented
> in Step 0e-bis + Phase 2 below. Use its `scripts/tts-wittgenstein.js` as the
> prosody-aware TTS template (the ep4 `tts-ep4.js` template is prosody-free).

Two audio source paths are now supported:
- **Audio given** — raw per-act MP3/WAV handed to you (ep2, ep3).
- **TTS-generated** — narration script → Edge TTS → per-chunk MP3s → concat → WAV (ep4).

Both paths converge at the same STT → subs → Remotion pipeline from Phase 1 onward.

> Companion docs: `.cursor/rules/subtitles.md` (authoritative subtitle
> pipeline + locked renderer contract), `.cursor/rules/chunks.md` (write code
> in chunks). Read both before starting.

## What you end up with (file map)

```
my-video/
├── public/projects/<project>/
│   ├── <narration>.md                # authoritative narration (given or written)
│   ├── tts_chunks/{actId}/           # TTS path only — per-chunk mp3 + txt + chunks.json
│   ├── {actId}.mp3                   # per-act MP3 — STT source (given or TTS-concat)
│   ├── {actId}.wav                   # delay-free WAV — what compositions PLAY
│   ├── words/{actId}.words.json      # STT word-level timing (Phase 1)
│   ├── transcripts/{actId}.{srt,txt} # STT transcripts (Phase 1)
│   └── subs/{actId}.subs.json        # built subs, inspection copy (Phase 2)
├── scripts/
│   ├── tts-<project>.js              # TTS path — chunks narration + calls Edge TTS
│   │                                 #   (prosody path: split on **bold**, per-segment
│   │                                 #    pitch/rate, joinBeat, baked [停顿] silence)
│   ├── concat-<project>-audio.js     # TTS path only — stitches chunks → MP3 + WAV
│   ├── transcribe-<project>.js       # Phase 1 — uploads WAV to Whisper
│   ├── fix-<project>-actN-tail.js    # Phase 1 — tail-truncation splice (dynamic OFFSET)
│   ├── build-<project>-subs.js       # Phase 2 — aligns timing + builds subs
│   └── check-<project>-subs.js       # Phase 2 validation (monotonic sweep)
└── src/projects/<project>/
    ├── theme.ts  Particles.tsx  LowPoly.tsx
    ├── SceneShell.tsx                 # shell chrome + audio + ambient + subs
    ├── acts.ts                        # FPS, intro/tail frames, AUDIO_LEN_SEC, actDuration()
    ├── Open.tsx  Act1.tsx..ActN.tsx   # one composition per act
    └── scenes/
        ├── _subtitles.tsx             # <EduSubtitles> renderer
        └── _subs/
            ├── _types.ts
            └── {actId}.subs.ts        # AUTO-GENERATED — never hand-edit
```

Source files are also registered in `src/Root.tsx` (Phase 4). MP3s are kept
(scripts reference them); compositions play the WAVs.

> **Local TTS server (prosody path).** The Edge TTS MCP runs from
> `MCPs/tts/index.js` on `localhost:3100`. To support `rate`/`pitch`/`volume`
> it was edited to forward those to `edge-tts-universal`'s `ProsodyOptions`
> (and exposed in the `tts_synthesize` zod schema + the `/api/tts/synthesize`
> REST route). If you re-clone or reset that server, re-apply that change or
> per-segment prosody silently no-ops.

## Decide the track shape first (this drives everything)

| Audio layout            | Reference                            | Pipeline                                                                                                                              |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **One continuous file** | `6-time-money`                       | single `words.json`, one track `actId:"full"`, acts play **slices** via `<Audio startFrom/endAt>` + `frameOffset`/window on subtitles |
| **One file per act**    | `5-education-ep2`, `7-education-ep3`, `11-education-ep4`, `13-wittgenstein-ai` | one `words.json` **per act**, one subtitle track per `actId`, each act plays its file **WHOLE** (no slicing)          |

Pick the shape that matches the audio you were handed. ep7/ep4 had per-act files
so they use the per-act model (ep2 plumbing) while borrowing #6's visual register.

Even when the audio is per-act, you still **mix two references**:

- **Visual register / look** → copy from `6-time-money` (hero desk image,
  terrain, particles, chapter-title intro, 1440×1080 @ 60fps spec).
- **Subtitle plumbing** → copy from `5-education-ep2` (per-act `SUBS_BY_ACT`,
  simple `actId` prop, no `frameOffset`/window machinery).
- **Auto-emphasis** (only if the narration is PLAIN / has no `[强调]` tags) →
  copy `autoTagEmphases()` + `EMPHASIS_KEYWORDS` from
  `build-timemoney-subs.js`. If the narration already carries `[强调]` tags,
  use the tag-driven path from `build-eduEp2-subs.js` instead.

## ⚠️ The MP3 playback trap (read this — it bites every time)

**Remotion compositions must play WAV, not MP3.** MP3 carries encoder
delay/padding (a non-zero `start_time`, e.g. `0.025057`) and has coarse seek
granularity. When an act plays its audio inside `<Sequence from={120}>` (the
2-second chapter-title intro), Remotion seeks into the MP3, lands just before
the true start, and **replays the head of the file** — you hear the first
phrase, it repeats, then continues normally.

This is NOT a code bug and NOT a corrupt audio file — the file is fine; it's
the format. #6 dodges this by rendering from a WAV master and using MP3 only
for transcription.

**Fix:** convert each per-act file to a delay-free WAV and point the
compositions at the WAV. Keep the MP3s — the transcribe/build scripts still
reference them.

```bash
for f in act_cold act_1 act_2 act_3 act_4; do
  ffmpeg -y -i "public/projects/<project>/$f.mp3" \
    -ar 48000 -ac 2 -c:a pcm_s16le \
    "public/projects/<project>/$f.wav" -loglevel error
done
# verify start_time is gone (prints N/A for WAV):
ffprobe -v error -show_entries stream=start_time \
  -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 public/projects/<project>/act_1.wav
```

Then set every `<SceneShell audioPath="…">` to the `.wav`. WAVs are tracked
via **Git LFS** (`.gitattributes: *.wav filter=lfs`), so a multi-hundred-MB
master won't bloat the repo — confirm with `git check-ignore` /
`git check-attr filter <file>` before committing.

> Diagnostic shortcut: if a user reports "the audio repeats the first phrase
> then plays normally," it's almost always this. Check `ffprobe … start_time`
> on the MP3; if non-zero, convert to WAV. The transcript/`words.json` starting
> cleanly (single occurrence) confirms the content is fine and the artifact is
> playback-only.

## Phase 0 (TTS path only) — Script preparation + TTS generation

Skip this phase entirely if you have real recorded audio. Use this path when
generating audio from a written narration script using Edge TTS.

### Step 0a — Prepare the narration for speech

Before generating TTS, the script must be edited for speech flow. Written
prose and spoken audio have different requirements — what reads well on screen
sounds broken when spoken aloud.

**Common issues to fix:**

1. **Staccato fragments** — short standalone sentences separated by periods
   that TTS reads as complete stops with long silences between them. Merge into
   flowing prose joined by commas, em-dashes, or conjunctions.

   ```
   ❌  你烧掉的十几年。退不回来的年华。
       你签在纸上的那个名字。一具能被问责、能被开除的肉身。

   ✓   你烧掉的十几年，退不回来的年华；你签在纸上的那个名字，
       一具能被问责、能被开除的肉身；...
   ```

2. **List items as periods** — lists where each item ends with `。` get read as
   separate sentences with full stops. Use `；` between items and `。` only at
   the end of the full list, or join with `、` for short items.

3. **Very short paragraphs** — a single sentence paragraph surrounded by blank
   lines causes TTS to insert a long pause before and after. Merge with the
   surrounding paragraph unless the pause is intentional (e.g. `[停顿]`).

4. **Markdown bold** — `**text**` markers have TWO possible roles depending on
   the TTS template:
   - **Prosody-free path (ep4):** `**` is stripped for TTS by `stripMarkdown()`
     and stripped for display in the build script. On-screen emphasis comes
     only from `EMPHASIS_KEYWORDS` auto-tagging; the `**` markers are inert.
   - **Prosody path (#13):** `**bold**` spans are the author's dramatic beats
     and are REPURPOSED as per-segment audio prosody (see Step 0e-bis). The TTS
     script splits each chunk on `**` and synthesizes the bold span as its own
     Edge TTS call at a lower pitch / slower rate. They are STILL stripped for
     on-screen display; subtitle emphasis is still keyword-driven, independent
     of `**`.

5. **Pause markers** — `[停顿]` and `[长停顿]` again have two roles:
   - **Prosody-free path (ep4):** stripped by `stripMarkdown()`, do NOT affect
     TTS timing — natural pauses come from punctuation only.
   - **Prosody path (#13):** they are FLOW CONTROL. The TTS script turns each
     into a forced chunk boundary with baked-in trailing silence (`[停顿]`
     ≈ 180ms, `[长停顿]` ≈ 450ms) appended to the preceding chunk's mp3. They
     are still stripped from spoken text and from the display text, but the
     silence is real audio. This is how you honor the script's authored pacing
     when the source is TTS rather than a human narrator.

**Rule of thumb:** read each paragraph aloud before chunking. If you naturally
pause mid-sentence because of a period, that period needs to become a comma.

### Step 0b — Section heading normalization

Section headings can contain curly quotes (`"认知"`) that break JS string
literal matching. Always normalize heading lookup with:

```js
function normalizeHeading(s) {
  return s
    .replace(/\u201c/g, '"').replace(/\u201d/g, '"') // " " → "
    .replace(/\u2018/g, "'").replace(/\u2019/g, "'") // ' ' → '
    .trim();
}
// Build lookup map with normalized keys
const SECTION_TO_ACT = {};
for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(SECTION_TO_ACT_RAW)) {
  SECTION_TO_ACT[normalizeHeading(k)] = v;
}
// Look up with normalization
const actId = SECTION_TO_ACT[normalizeHeading(section.title)];
```

This is critical — a heading mismatch silently produces zero chunks for that
act with no error.

### Step 0c — Pronunciation substitutions (polyphone handling)

Chinese has many polyphonic characters (多音字) — characters with two or more
valid pronunciations. TTS models default to the most statistically common
reading, which is often wrong for specialized vocabulary.

**How to fix:** before sending text to TTS, substitute the problematic
character with a homophone that has only one reading. The substitution is
TTS-only — the displayed subtitle text uses the original correct character
(stored separately in `displayText` vs `ttsText`).

**Pattern:**

```js
const PRONUNCIATION_SUBS = [
  // [text-to-replace, replacement-for-TTS-only]
  // Order: longer/more-specific patterns first
  ["重装税",   "崇装税"],   // 重 here = chóng (reinstall), not zhòng (heavy)
  ["重装成本", "崇装成本"],
  ["每代重装", "每代崇装"],
  ["重装",     "崇装"],     // catch-all last
];
```

**Substitution logic:**
- Find a homophone with the correct pronunciation and no ambiguity.
- The substituted character should be rare enough that it won't appear
  naturally in the narration (to avoid false positives).
- After TTS generates the audio, the `chunks.json` manifest stores BOTH the
  `ttsText` (sent to TTS) and `displayText` (shown on screen) for each chunk.

**Common polyphones to watch for:**

| Character | Wrong default | Correct in context | Fix |
|-----------|--------------|-------------------|-----|
| 重 | zhòng (heavy) | chóng (re-, again) | 崇 |
| 长 | cháng (long) | zhǎng (grow/chief) | depends on context |
| 行 | xíng (walk/OK) | háng (row/trade) | 杭 or 航 |
| 的 | de (particle) | dí (target) | context usually fine |
| 中 | zhōng (middle) | zhòng (hit/be affected) | depends |
| 处 | chǔ (handle) | chù (place) | depends |

When reviewing TTS output: if a word sounds wrong, it's almost always a
polyphonic character. Identify the correct pinyin, find a homophone substitute,
add it to `PRONUNCIATION_SUBS`, regenerate that chunk only with `--act X --chunk N`.

### Step 0d — TTS chunking strategy

The TTS script splits narration into ~280-char chunks per act. Key rules:

**Chunk size:** ~280 chars soft target, 350 hard max. This balances:
- Avoiding TTS timeout/quality degradation on very long inputs
- Avoiding excessive silence and unnatural pacing on very short inputs
- Enabling surgical regeneration of individual chunks after edits

**Break priority order:**
1. Hard sentence-end punctuation: `。！？!?` (include the punctuation in the chunk)
2. Em-dash pair: `——`
3. Soft punctuation: `，、：；,;:`
4. Fallback: cut at soft max

**Merge tiny trailing chunks:** chunks under 20 chars get merged into the
previous chunk. Avoids TTS generating a tiny file for a sentence fragment.

**Per-chunk output:**
```
tts_chunks/{actId}/
  chunk-000.mp3    ← TTS audio
  chunk-000.txt    ← ttsText sent to TTS (with substitutions applied)
  chunks.json      ← manifest: [{idx, ttsText, displayText, file}]
```

The manifest is what makes surgical regeneration possible. To regenerate a
single chunk after a narration edit:
```bash
node scripts/tts-<project>.js --act act3 --chunk 7 --force
node scripts/concat-<project>-audio.js act3
node scripts/transcribe-<project>.js act3
node scripts/build-<project>-subs.js
```

### Step 0e — TTS MCP

The TTS MCP is at `http://localhost:3100/mcp`. Tool name: `tts_synthesize`.

```js
// SSE-based HTTP call (not standard fetch — needs Accept header)
const body = JSON.stringify({
  jsonrpc: "2.0", id: Date.now(), method: "tools/call",
  params: {
    name: "tts_synthesize",
    arguments: { text, voice: "zh-CN-YunyangNeural", output_path: chunkPath }
  }
});
// Headers required:
//   Content-Type: application/json
//   Accept: application/json, text/event-stream
// Parse SSE response: lines starting with "data: " contain the JSON result
```

Voice: `zh-CN-YunyangNeural` — male, authoritative, suitable for educational
narration. Add a 100ms delay between calls to avoid hammering the service.

**⚠️ Inline `<prosody>` SSML does NOT work — it is spoken LITERALLY.** The Edge
TTS MCP escapes your text into its own SSML wrapper, so a tag like
`<prosody pitch="+15Hz" rate="-15%">重要</prosody>` comes out of the voice as
the spoken words "prosody pitch 等于 加 15Hz rate 等于…". Verified on #13. Do
NOT try to inject per-word SSML into the `text` argument.

**What DOES work (whole-utterance prosody params).** The local MCP server
(`MCPs/tts/index.js`) was extended to forward `rate` / `pitch` / `volume` to
`edge-tts-universal`'s `ProsodyOptions` (the lib supports them; the server just
wasn't passing them). The `tts_synthesize` tool now accepts:

```js
arguments: {
  text, voice: "zh-CN-YunyangNeural", output_path,
  rate: "-15%",   // explicit Edge rate string (takes precedence over `speed`)
  pitch: "-6Hz",  // whole-utterance pitch shift
  volume: "+0%",  // whole-utterance volume
}
```

These apply to the WHOLE call. To vary prosody WITHIN a sentence you must split
the sentence into separate TTS calls and stitch them (Step 0e-bis). If you
restart the TTS server to pick up server-side edits, launch it as a persistent
background process (it dies when a transient shell session ends) and confirm
`GET /health` returns `{"status":"ok"}` before generating.

### Step 0e-bis — Segment-level prosody + dramatic beats (#13 prosody path)

When the narration carries `**bold**` dramatic beats and you want them AUDIBLE
(not just colored on screen), use the #13 model: synthesize each chunk as a
sequence of SEGMENTS and concatenate, instead of one flat TTS call.

1. **Split each chunk on `**`** into alternating normal / emphasized segments.
2. **Synthesize each segment separately** — emphasized segments get
   `EMPHASIS_PROSODY` (#13 used `{ rate: "-10%", pitch: "-6Hz" }` for a
   weightier, leaned-in delivery); normal segments get default prosody.
3. **Trim + beat the inner joins, then concatenate** (see the join logic below).
4. **Append `[停顿]` silence** to the last segment if the chunk ends on a pause
   marker.

**⚠️ The "No audio was received" trap.** Splitting on `**` can leave a segment
that is ONLY punctuation (e.g. a lone `。` after `**生活形式**`). Edge TTS
returns "No audio was received" for a non-speakable segment and the chunk
fails. FIX: in `parseSegments()`, merge any segment with no
`[\u3400-\u9fff0-9A-Za-z]` char into its neighbor (it keeps the neighbor's
prosody; trailing punctuation is inaudible anyway). Also wrap each TTS call in
a small retry (`ttsSynthesizeRetry`, 2 tries) for transient blips.

**⚠️ The dramatic setup→reveal beat (the #13 "rushed reveal" bug).** Edge TTS
pads each segment with ~0.2–0.3s lead and ~0.5–0.7s trailing silence. If you
naively trim every inner join flat (~70ms), you DELETE exactly the dramatic
pause before a reveal — `标题就四个字——言尽意论` / `没有争议：言…` sound rushed
and unnatural (this was the user-reported regression on #13). FIX: trim each
inner join tight to kill Edge's variable padding, then APPEND a deliberate,
contextual beat so the gap is guaranteed:

```js
// joinBeat(prevText, nextSeg) → seconds of silence appended at the join.
// Take the MAX of the applicable signals so a reveal always gets its lean-in.
const JOIN_BEAT_REVEAL = 0.32; // next segment is an emphasized reveal
const JOIN_BEAT_SETUP  = 0.26; // prev segment ends with —— or ：/: (setup→reveal)
const JOIN_BEAT_HARD   = 0.20; // prev ends 。！？ mid-chunk
const JOIN_BEAT_SOFT   = 0.05; // prev ends ，、；  (keep tight, mid-clause split)
// trim: INNER_TRIM_LEAD 0.03 on every non-first segment; tail trimmed tight
// (0.04) on every non-last segment; chunk OUTER edges left untouched so the
// chunk-to-chunk breath stays natural.
```

These four `JOIN_BEAT_*` constants at the top of the TTS script are the single
pacing knob — tune them, regenerate, done. A segment join carrying a real beat
measures ~0.39s of silence (vs the crushed ~0.07s) on `ffmpeg silencedetect`.

**⚠️ Initials get read as letters.** A bare Latin initial in prose (e.g. "早期W"
meaning 早期维特根斯坦) is spoken as the English letter "W" — jarring. Write the
full name in the narration. (Initials inside a stripped `> 结构` blockquote are
fine — they never reach audio.) After fixing, regenerate just that chunk:
`node scripts/tts-<project>.js --act actN --chunk K --force`.

### Step 0f — Concat chunks → per-act WAV

Use ffmpeg concat demuxer to stitch all chunks in order:

```js
// Write concat list
const listContent = chunkFiles.map(f => `file '${f}'`).join('\n');
fs.writeFileSync(listPath, listContent);

// Step 1: concat MP3s (kept for STT)
execSync(`ffmpeg -y -f concat -safe 0 -i "${listPath}" -c copy "${mp3Out}"`);

// Step 2: MP3 → delay-free WAV (the format Remotion plays)
execSync(`ffmpeg -y -i "${mp3Out}" -ar 48000 -ac 2 -c:a pcm_s16le "${wavOut}"`);

// Verify: start_time must be N/A
execSync(`ffprobe -v error -show_entries stream=start_time ... "${wavOut}"`);
```

Always verify `start_time=N/A` on the output WAV before proceeding.

### Step 0g — Master the per-act WAVs (loudness normalization)

Raw TTS output is quiet and inconsistent across acts (#13 measured ~-26.8 LUFS
integrated, but true peaks only ~-5.6 dBTP — a huge ~21 dB crest factor because
the `[停顿]` pauses and dramatic beats pull the average down). Master each act
to a consistent broadcast loudness so playback is even and platform-ready.

Use the transparent profile script (highpass 80Hz + transient limiter + a
**two-pass linear loudnorm**, NO compression/denoise):

```bash
SH=/Users/jingyu/.cursor/skills/audio-processing/master_to_wav.sh
cd public/projects/<project>
for a in open act1 act2 act3 act4 act5 act6 act7 outro; do
  o=$(ffprobe -v error -show_entries format=duration -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 "$a.wav")
  TARGET_I=-14 TARGET_TP=-1 TARGET_LRA=7 "$SH" "$a.wav" "/tmp/master/$a.wav"
  m=$(ffprobe -v error -show_entries format=duration -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 "/tmp/master/$a.wav")
  # CRITICAL: duration MUST be unchanged (subtitle timing + AUDIO_LEN_SEC depend on it)
  node -e "process.exit(Math.abs($o-$m) > 0.01 ? 1 : 0)" || echo "  ⚠ $a DURATION DRIFT — do NOT swap"
done
# only after every act passes the duration check, swap the mastered files in:
for a in open act1 act2 act3 act4 act5 act6 act7 outro; do cp "/tmp/master/$a.wav" "$a.wav"; done
```

**Targets & the "as loud as possible without distorting" reality.**
- loudnorm + highpass + alimiter are all **duration-preserving**, so mastering
  AFTER concat (and re-mastering after any audio edit) is safe — but ALWAYS
  verify `durΔ < 0.01s` per act before swapping, or subtitle sync breaks.
- A target of **-14 LUFS @ -1 dBTP** is the transparent sweet spot for this kind
  of dynamic narration (and the de-facto YouTube/Bilibili playback target).
- Asking for **-13 LUFS** on this material lands at ~-14 LUFS in transparent
  mode — that's correct, not a failure. To force a true -13 under a -1 dBTP
  ceiling, loudnorm would have to LIMIT ~9 dB of speech peaks, which is exactly
  the crushing the transparent profile avoids. "As close as possible without
  distorting" == ~-14 here. To go genuinely louder you'd raise `TARGET_TP` to
  `-0.5`, or switch to a compressing (non-transparent) profile — both trade
  away dynamics; only do it on explicit request.
- `TARGET_LRA=7` (vs the script default 5) leaves the dramatic-beat dynamics
  intact; tighten toward 5 only if an act feels too uneven.

**Post-master verify (re-confirm the WAV contract after swap):**
- `start_time=N/A` (loudnorm export stays clean — #13 confirmed).
- format `48000 / 2ch / 24-bit` PCM — note the master script exports
  **24-bit** (the concat step wrote 16-bit); both play fine in Remotion and
  stay Git-LFS tracked.
- spot-check integrated LUFS with
  `ffmpeg -i <a>.wav -af loudnorm=I=-14:print_format=json -f null -`.
- Mastering does NOT change durations, so `AUDIO_LEN_SEC` and the subtitles do
  NOT need rebuilding — but if you mastered as part of an audio-content edit
  (re-concat), the normal re-transcribe/rebuild still applies.

## Phase 1 — Speech-to-text (timing only)

The narration markdown is **authoritative for displayed text**; STT supplies
**timing only**. Mishearings never reach the screen (fixed by construction in
Phase 2).

Adapt `scripts/transcribe-eduEp2.js` → `scripts/transcribe-<project>.js`:

- Point `PROJECT_DIR` at the new project, `TARGETS` at the per-act WAV files.
- Request `language:chinese, word_timestamps:true`.
- `alignedAgainst` = the narration filename.

Per act, emit (matching the ep2 schema):

- `public/projects/<project>/words/{actId}.words.json` —
  `{ source, durationSec, wordCount, alignedAgainst, words:[{word,start,end}] }`
- `public/projects/<project>/transcripts/{actId}.{srt,txt}`

**TTS-generated audio transcribes much cleaner than human narration.** Whisper
mishearings are rarer because the voice is consistent, pronunciation is
standard, and there are no fillers or disfluencies. The CONTEXTUAL_FOLDS table
for TTS-sourced projects is shorter — mainly digit rendering and a few
pronunciation-substitution reversals (e.g. fold `崇装` back to `重装` since the
TTS substitution should not affect the subtitle alignment cursor).

Notes:

- The script and the Speech-to-Text MCP hit the same Whisper service
  (`whisper-app-production-dc4b.up.railway.app`); either satisfies "use STT."
  The script is usually the more reliable route for a batch of files. If you
  use the MCP, still write the same `words.json`/srt/txt so the build script
  can consume them.
- Files <100MB upload directly. A very long act may hit a transient SSL error
  — re-run that act alone.
- Get **true** durations from the WAV: `ffprobe -v error -show_entries
  format=duration -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 file.wav`
  (VBR MP3 headers misreport; WAVs are accurate).
- For TTS-sourced audio: `durationSec` from `words.json` ≈ WAV duration from
  ffprobe. If they differ by more than 1s, re-check chunk concat order.

### ⚠️ The Whisper tail-truncation trap (silently drops the last sentence)

Symptom the user reports: *"the act ends abruptly before the last sentence is
spoken"* / *"the final subtitle is cut off."* On long acts (>~5min) Whisper
sometimes **stops transcribing several seconds early** and returns a `words.json`
whose last word ends well before the WAV does. This is NOT a transient error —
**re-running the full act transcription reproduces the same truncation.** It bit
ep4 act5: words ended at 338.1s, WAV was 342.9s — the entire closing sentence
was missing from `words.json`.

Two downstream failures, both silent:
1. The composition duration in `acts.ts` (`AUDIO_LEN_SEC`) inherits the
   truncated `durationSec`, so the composition ends before the audio finishes —
   the last sentence plays past the end and gets cut.
2. The final-sentence subtitle chunks have no words to anchor to, so they fall
   back to a bogus timestamp near the truncation point.

**Detection (run after every transcribe):** compare `words.json` `durationSec`
against the true WAV duration. A gap >1.5s on any act means truncation.

```bash
for a in open act1 act2 act3 act4 act5 act6 act7; do
  wav=$(ffprobe -v error -show_entries format=duration -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 "public/projects/<project>/$a.wav")
  dur=$(node -e "console.log(JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('public/projects/<project>/words/$a.words.json')).durationSec)")
  echo "$a  words=$dur  wav=$wav"
done
```

**Fix (reliable for TTS-concatenated audio — chunk boundaries are known
exactly):** transcribe the **final TTS chunk alone** (short clips don't
truncate), offset its word timings to the chunk's WAV position, and splice them
in over the truncated tail. **Compute the offset DYNAMICALLY** — durations shift
on every regeneration, so a hardcoded number silently mis-times the spliced tail
(this bit #13 when a beat-edit regen changed act3's length):

```js
const { execSync } = require("child_process");
const ffdur = (p) => parseFloat(execSync(
  `ffprobe -v error -show_entries format=duration -of default=noprint_wrappers=1:nokey=1 "${p}"`,
  { encoding: "utf8" }).trim());
// final chunk's WAV start = full act WAV duration − final chunk duration
const OFFSET = +(ffdur(`${proj}/act3.wav`) - ffdur(`${proj}/tts_chunks/act3/chunk-034.mp3`)).toFixed(3);
const head = main.filter(w => w.start < OFFSET);          // keep chunks 0..N-1
const tail = chunkWords.map(w => ({                        // standalone re-transcribe
  word: w.word, start: +(w.start + OFFSET).toFixed(3), end: +(w.end + OFFSET).toFixed(3),
}));
data.words = head.concat(tail);
data.wordCount = data.words.length;
data.durationSec = tail[tail.length - 1].end;              // now matches the WAV
```

Then **update `AUDIO_LEN_SEC[actId]` in `acts.ts`** to the new `durationSec` and
rebuild subs. (For human-recorded audio there is no chunk to re-transcribe in
isolation — instead clip the tail with ffmpeg `-ss`, transcribe the clip, and
splice the same way.) NOTE: truncation is format/length-driven, not random — it
RECURS on the same act after a regen, so re-check + re-splice every time.

## Phase 2 — Build subtitles + fix transcription errors

Create `scripts/build-<project>-subs.js` by combining:

- the **per-act, multi-section** loop of `build-eduEp2-subs.js` (iterate each
  `## ` section, align against that act's `words.json`, cursor resets per act;
  `### ` subsection headings are scaffolding — skip the heading line, keep the
  body), and
- the **auto-emphasis** path of `build-timemoney-subs.js` if the narration is
  plain (port `autoTagEmphases()` + a fresh `EMPHASIS_KEYWORDS` table).

Also copy `scenes/_subs/_types.ts` from a reference project.

**Map the narration's `## ` headings to actIds.** The build script needs an
explicit `SECTION_TO_ACT` object keyed by the EXACT heading text (without the
`## `). Get the headings with `grep -n "^## " <narration>.md`, then map each to
its audio file's actId. Always use the normalizeHeading() pattern (see Phase 0b)
— curly quotes in headings silently break the lookup. ep4's map:

```js
const SECTION_TO_ACT_RAW = {
  "Cold Open（冷开场——拆包裹隐喻 + 去拟人化论点 + 问题重定向）": "open",
  "Setup — 那些我们说过的，和没说的（连接前3集 + 尊重旧模型）": "act1",
  "Movement 1 — 认知值钱，因为它是不可复制的": "act2",
  'Movement 2 — 那份安排，从来没有在给"认知"定价': "act3",
  // ...
};
const SECTION_TO_ACT = {};
for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(SECTION_TO_ACT_RAW)) {
  SECTION_TO_ACT[normalizeHeading(k)] = v;
}
```

A typo or quote mismatch here means a whole act silently produces zero chunks —
verify chunk counts per act after building.

**Strip markdown formatting in `processBlock`.** The narration may contain
`**bold**` markers for visual emphasis. These must be stripped before the
display text is built — otherwise asterisks appear verbatim in on-screen
subtitles. Add this at the top of `processBlock()`:

```js
function processBlock(blockText) {
  // Strip markdown bold (**text** → text) before processing
  const cleanedBlock = blockText.replace(/\*\*([^*]+)\*\*/g, "$1");
  // ... rest of function uses cleanedBlock instead of blockText
}
```

The `**` stripping only affects the displayed subtitle text. Emphasis on screen
is driven by `EMPHASIS_KEYWORDS` auto-tagging — the `**` markers in the
narration are irrelevant to the subtitle emphasis system.

**Display-only punctuation strip (em dashes).** To clean the on-screen text
without touching audio, word timing, or alignment, strip chars AFTER alignment
but BEFORE emphasis is computed — right where each chunk's final `text` is set:

```js
for (const c of result.chunks) {
  // Display-only: drop em dashes. They are non-canonical, so the alignment
  // cursor already ignores them — this changes NOTHING in audio/timing.
  // Strip BEFORE autoTagEmphases so emphasis offsets match the shown text.
  c.text = c.text.replace(/—/g, "");
  c.emphases = autoTagEmphases(c.text);
}
```

This is the safe place for any display-only cleanup (em dashes on #13): it runs
on the post-alignment text, so the monotonic sweep stays clean and only the
build step needs re-running (no re-concat / re-transcribe). Order matters —
strip first, THEN compute emphasis, or the colored-span offsets drift.

**Frame geometry must match the project's frame**, not the reference script's.
ep4/ep7 are 1440 wide (4:3) like #6, so use #6's width-driven ladder
(`60/56/52/48/44/40`, default 60, slot ≈0.84 of 1440) — NOT ep2's 1920
settings. Match whatever the renderer's `widthPct` is.

Emit:

- `public/projects/<project>/subs/{actId}.subs.json` (inspection)
- `src/projects/<project>/scenes/_subs/{actId}.subs.ts` (Remotion runtime)

### Emphasis (phrase-level only, never whole-line)

Build an `EMPHASIS_KEYWORDS` table from the episode's actual argument
primitives (read the narration). Semantic roles:

- `insight` (cyan-green) — the model / resolution vocabulary.
- `tension` (amber) — the problem being diagnosed.
- `system` (steel blue) — cited thinkers, taxonomy labels, baseline quantities.

Match shortest/most-specific first, non-overlapping (e.g. `延伸者` before
`延伸`). Consistent recurring-primitive coloring is intentional — it's how the
channel's chunks crystallize across the runtime. Exposition without argument
load gets no emphasis.

### Confusable-fold table = transcription-error correction

Whisper mishears words and renders Chinese numerals as Arabic digits. Because
alignment strips non-CJK chars, an unfolded `"2025"` collapses to nothing and
the cursor loses its place. `CONTEXTUAL_FOLDS` maps **wrong (STT) form →
narration's exact characters** so alignment lands correctly. Order longer
patterns first. Categories that always need attention:

- **Chinese numerals (critical):** scan the narration for EVERY spoken number
  and fold it — years (`2025→二零二五`), percents/ages
  (`16→十六`, `21→二十一`), decimals (`2.1→二点一`, `1.0→一点零`,
  `13.3→十三点三`), big numbers (`150→一百五十`, `580→五百八十`).
- **Traditional-variant emission:** Whisper intermittently emits Traditional
  Chinese mid-stream. A single-char `SAFE_FOLDS` map (學→学, 這→这, 個→个,
  讀→读, 紅樓夢→红楼梦, …) clears most "block-not-found" warnings. This was the
  single biggest fix on ep7.
- **Recurring-primitive homophones:** the core terms recur hundreds of times,
  so one wrong fold costs many chunks. ep7 example: `主块/主快→组块` (~50× in
  act1). Also `不可逆性睡→不可逆性税`, `竖感→数感`, inanimate `他→它` in
  specific phrases.
- **Proper nouns:** seed anchors (e.g. 柏拉图, 苏格拉底, 卡尼曼, 塞勒,
  布林约尔松, 古德哈特, 斯坦福).

When a future narration edit introduces a new mishearing, add a
`["wrong","right"]` pair and rebuild. **Never hand-edit `{actId}.subs.ts`** —
edit the narration or the build tables and regenerate.

#### ⚠️ The homophone cursor-jump trap (the #1 subtitle bug — read this)

This is the single most common alignment failure and it is **silent** — no
build warning fires. Symptom the user reports: *"the subtitle doesn't show from
phrase X onward"* or *"a whole paragraph is missing / blacked out"* or *"the
subtitles jump ahead then come back."*

**Mechanism.** Even on clean TTS audio, Whisper transcribes a character as its
**homophone** (它/他, 集/级, 合上/和尚 — all valid same-sound readings). The
narration chunk's literal prefix then can't match at its true position, so the
aligner's fallback (a short 3–4 char prefix anchor) matches a **later,
identical-looking phrase** instead. The cursor jumps forward — often tens of
seconds — dragging an entire paragraph of chunks with it, colliding with later
chunks and producing degenerate (zero-length) and out-of-order entries. Nothing
renders for the displaced span.

This bit ep4 in **four** places (one per act 3/4/5/6), all the same shape:

| Act | STT heard | Narration | Displacement |
|-----|-----------|-----------|--------------|
| act4 | `第二级` | `第二集` | 第一样 paragraph (~40s) blacked out |
| act5 | `他是一个招募` | `它是一个招募` | 招募 paragraph dragged ~37s forward |
| act3 | `但他没有生命` | `但它没有生命` | 但它没有生命/所以… displaced |
| act6 | `和尚`, `之前他吞掉` | `合上`, `之前它吞掉` | 空位期 paragraph jumped ~14s |

**Fix recipe** (each fold verified safe — see below):
1. Inspect the audio words around the gap:
   `node -e 'JSON.parse(...words.json).words` … print words in the suspect
   time window joined as a string. The mishearing is usually obvious.
2. Confirm the STT form is **never legitimate narration** (count it across all
   `words.json` AND in the narration `.md`). A fold is only safe if the wrong
   form appears solely as the mishearing.
3. Add a `CONTEXTUAL_FOLD` mapping the **STT form → narration's characters**.
   Prefer the **longest specific phrase** (`他是一个招募`, not a bare `他→它`)
   so the fold can't over-match elsewhere. Folds apply on the raw STT string
   left-to-right, so a phrase fold like `之前他吞掉` works even when a broader
   fold (`和尚→合上`) also fires in the same block.
4. Rebuild and re-run the monotonic sweep (see Validate) until `nonmono:0`.

**它→他 (inanimate referent) is the recurring offender** across every episode —
TTS narration personifies abstractions ("它" = the pressure/system/arrangement)
and Whisper writes the animate homophone. Seed the fold table with the specific
它-phrases from the new narration up front.

### Validate

Create/run `scripts/check-<project>-subs.js` (adapt `check-timemoney-subs.js`).

- **Real bugs → fix with folds:** multi-second overlaps, out-of-order chunks.
- **Benign → leave:** 1–2s display gaps that coincide with real narration
  pauses (renderer clears during silence), and sub-second overlaps within the
  8f-in/12f-out fade window.

**Always run the monotonic sweep after every build** — it catches the homophone
cursor-jump trap (above) that produces no build warning. Three signals, all of
which mean a cursor jumped:

- **non-monotonic `startFrame`** — a chunk starts earlier than the one before it
- **degenerate chunks** — `endFrame <= startFrame` (zero-length, invisible)
- **suspiciously long single chunks** — one line spanning >6.5s usually means it
  mis-anchored and got stretched by interpolation

```js
// scripts/check-<project>-subs.js — run across ALL acts, fail loud on anomalies
const dir = "public/projects/<project>/subs";
for (const f of fs.readdirSync(dir).filter(f => f.endsWith(".subs.json"))) {
  const c = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(dir + "/" + f, "utf8")).chunks;
  let bad = 0, prev = -1, badIdx = [];
  c.forEach((x, i) => { if (x.startFrame < prev) { bad++; badIdx.push(x.chunkId); } prev = x.startFrame; });
  const deg = c.filter(x => x.endFrame <= x.startFrame).length;
  const long = c.filter(x => (x.endSec - x.startSec) > 6.5).map(x => x.chunkId);
  console.log(f, "nonmono:" + bad, "degen:" + deg, "long:" + (long.join(",") || "-"),
    badIdx.length ? "  jumps@" + badIdx.join(",") : "");
}
```

A clean episode reads `nonmono:0 degen:0 long:-` on every act. Any non-zero
value points at the exact chunk where a fold is needed.

### Regenerate commands

```bash
# TTS path — full pipeline
node scripts/tts-<project>.js                    # generate all chunks (skips existing)
node scripts/tts-<project>.js --act act3 --chunk 7 --force  # regenerate one chunk
node scripts/concat-<project>-audio.js           # stitch chunks → MP3 + WAV (all acts)
node scripts/concat-<project>-audio.js act3      # one act only
node scripts/transcribe-<project>.js             # upload WAVs to Whisper (all acts)
node scripts/transcribe-<project>.js act3        # one act only

# Both paths — subtitle rebuild (run after ANY narration or fold-table edit)
node scripts/build-<project>-subs.js             # all acts
node scripts/check-<project>-subs.js             # validation
```

## Phase 3 — Per-act compositions (background + terrain + particles + subs)

Create `src/projects/<project>/` by copying #6's register:

- `theme.ts` — re-export `COLORS, FONT` from `../the-filter/theme`; export
  `FPS=60`, `LOGICAL_WIDTH`, `LOGICAL_HEIGHT`.
- `Particles.tsx` — re-export from `../the-filter/Particles`.
- `LowPoly.tsx` — copy #6's (`GlowFilters`, `terrainPoints`).
- `SceneShell.tsx` — model on **ep2's** SceneShell (per-act: `actId` +
  `audioPath` props, plays the whole file via `<Audio src={staticFile(audioPath)} />`,
  **no slicing / frameOffset / window**), keeping #6's chrome: hero desk image
  (`projects/the-migration/assets/cold-open/typography-v3/00-archive-table-top.png`),
  `HeroBackdrop` + `HeroVignette`, `ChapterTitle` (88px amber hero, mono
  kicker 0.18em, mono subtitle), `AmbientLayer` (terrain + particles) gated by
  an `ambient` prop. Subtitles render via `<EduSubtitles actId={actId} />`
  inside the post-intro `<Sequence from={chapterIntroFrames}>`.
- `scenes/_subtitles.tsx` — model on **ep2's** renderer (per-act
  `SUBS_BY_ACT = { open, act1, … }`, simple `actId` prop), keep the project's
  locked frame spec (#6: y=950, widthPct 0.84, Noto Serif SC 500/700, 0.04em,
  8f-in/12f-out fades, NC handling). In the per-act model each act's chunk
  `startFrame`/`endFrame` are **act-local** (chunk frame 0 = that act's audio
  onset). They stay in sync automatically because `<Audio>` and
  `<EduSubtitles>` are both mounted inside the SAME
  `<Sequence from={chapterIntroFrames}>`, so both share the act-local frame
  origin — no `frameOffset` needed.
- `acts.ts` — per-act: `FPS=60`, `CHAPTER_INTRO_FRAMES=120`, `TAIL_FRAMES=60`.
  Durations come from each act's `words.json` last-word end (`durationSec`).
  **`words.json` lives under `public/`, outside the src tsconfig include, so it
  can't be imported** — copy each act's `durationSec` into a hardcoded
  `AUDIO_LEN_SEC` map and compute `actDuration(actId,{intro})` =
  `intro + ceil(AUDIO_LEN_SEC[actId]*FPS) + TAIL_FRAMES` (open passes
  `intro:0`). Re-sync the map if audio is re-transcribed.
- `Open.tsx`, `Act1.tsx`…`ActN.tsx` — each renders `<SceneShell>` with the
  act's `actId`, `audioPath` (**.wav** — see the MP3 trap), `chapter` title,
  and exports a `*_DURATION` const.

### The opening-title / audio-delay contract (matches #6 + ep2)

- **Cold open:** `chapterIntroFrames={0}` — audio fires at frame 0, the title
  rides the live audio, hero image holds throughout.
- **Acts 1..N:** `chapterIntroFrames={120}` + `ambient` — the chapter title
  gets a solo **2-second** intro (120f @ 60fps); the hero image crossfades out
  across the last 30f; audio + subtitles + ambient layer start in lockstep at
  frame 120. This 120-frame `<Sequence from>` IS the "2s audio delay for the
  title transition" — it applies to every act EXCEPT the cold open.

For initial setup, acts need NO bespoke per-shot content — shell + terrain +
particles + subtitles is the correct base. Leave `children` empty; author
scenes later.

